From: Elie Bursztein <github@elie.net>
Subject: [PATCH] virtiofs: allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP

FUSE marks every superblock SB_I_NOIDMAP and clears it only when the
daemon negotiates FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP on a default_permissions mount. Once
cleared, fuse_get_req stops sending the caller's fsuid and fsgid: requests
that are not inode creation carry FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID, which only an
idmap-aware daemon accepts.

Apple's Virtualization.framework daemon never negotiates FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP
and ignores ownership altogether: every entry is 0:0 and chown is accepted
without effect. mount_setattr(MOUNT_ATTR_IDMAP) on the share therefore
failed with EINVAL, and a workload in a user namespace saw its workspace
owned by the overflow uid, so chown, tar -x and cp -a failed as container
root.

Separate the two decisions. virtio-fs, which always mounts with
default_permissions so the guest kernel makes every permission decision
through the mount's idmap, clears SB_I_NOIDMAP itself. Request headers
follow the idmap only when the daemon negotiated FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP
(fc->idmap_headers); every other daemon keeps receiving the caller's own
fsuid and fsgid, exactly as before. Clearing SB_I_NOIDMAP without this
sent Apple's daemon uid -1 on ordinary requests and it refused them, so
root could not create files in the share.

Nothing changes until a privileged process idmaps a mount explicitly; the
share's own mount keeps its identity mapping.

---
--- a/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
+++ b/fs/fuse/fuse_i.h
@@ -899,6 +899,9 @@
 	/** Passthrough support for read/write IO */
 	unsigned int passthrough:1;
 
+	/** Server negotiated FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP: request headers follow the idmap */
+	unsigned int idmap_headers:1;
+
 	/* Use pages instead of pointer for kernel I/O */
 	unsigned int use_pages_for_kvec_io:1;
 
--- a/fs/fuse/inode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/inode.c
@@ -1443,10 +1443,12 @@
 			if (flags & FUSE_NO_EXPORT_SUPPORT)
 				fm->sb->s_export_op = &fuse_export_fid_operations;
 			if (flags & FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP) {
-				if (fc->default_permissions)
+				if (fc->default_permissions) {
 					fm->sb->s_iflags &= ~SB_I_NOIDMAP;
-				else
+					fc->idmap_headers = 1;
+				} else {
 					ok = false;
+				}
 			}
 			if (flags & FUSE_OVER_IO_URING && fuse_uring_enabled())
 				fc->io_uring = 1;
--- a/fs/fuse/dev.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/dev.c
@@ -198,7 +198,14 @@
 {
 	struct fuse_conn *fc = fm->fc;
 	struct fuse_req *req;
-	bool no_idmap = !fm->sb || (fm->sb->s_iflags & SB_I_NOIDMAP);
+	/*
+	 * A server that did not negotiate FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP gets the caller's
+	 * fsuid and fsgid, as before idmapped mounts, even on a superblock
+	 * that allows them (virtio-fs). Only an idmap-aware server can take
+	 * FUSE_INVALID_UIDGID on requests that are not inode creation.
+	 */
+	bool no_idmap = !fm->sb || (fm->sb->s_iflags & SB_I_NOIDMAP) ||
+			!fc->idmap_headers;
 	kuid_t fsuid;
 	kgid_t fsgid;
 	int err;
--- a/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/virtio_fs.c
@@ -1584,6 +1584,19 @@
 	if (err < 0)
 		goto err_free_fuse_devs;
 
+	/*
+	 * Allow idmapped mounts without FUSE_ALLOW_IDMAP from the daemon.
+	 * virtio-fs always mounts with default_permissions, so the guest
+	 * kernel makes every permission decision through the mount's idmap,
+	 * and the daemon keeps receiving the caller's own fsuid and fsgid
+	 * (fuse_get_req). Apple's Virtualization.framework daemon never
+	 * negotiates the flag and reports every entry as uid 0, so without
+	 * this a user-namespaced workload cannot own its share. Only a mount
+	 * explicitly idmapped with mount_setattr changes.
+	 */
+	if (fc->default_permissions)
+		sb->s_iflags &= ~SB_I_NOIDMAP;
+
 	for (i = 0; i < fs->nvqs; i++) {
 		struct virtio_fs_vq *fsvq = &fs->vqs[i];
 
